VI(1)                          P32W Unix                           VI(1)

NAME
       vi - screen editor

SYNOPSIS
       vi [file]

DESCRIPTION
       vi is a screen editor in the manner of BSD vi. It shows a
       window of the file on a VT100-style terminal (the hardware
       serial terminals, the Mac's Terminal, nc) and edits it with
       single-key commands. The last row of the screen holds messages
       and the : / ? command lines.

       vi reads file into a buffer in memory and reports its size, or
       [New file] if it does not exist; changes reach the file only
       when it is written. With no file the buffer starts empty.

       vi starts in command mode, where keys move the cursor and
       change text. Insert commands enter insert mode, in which typed
       text goes into the buffer until ESC. Lines never wrap: the
       window scrolls sideways to show the cursor in a long line.
       Tabs stop every 8 columns; other control characters show as ?.
       Lines past the end of the buffer show as ~.

       Most commands take a count, typed before them in decimal; its
       meaning is given with each command. An unknown key rings the
       bell; ESC cancels a partly typed command.

MOVING
       h ^H left arrow
              left count characters
       l space right arrow
              right count characters
       j ^N down arrow
              down count lines, keeping the column
       k ^P up arrow
              up count lines, keeping the column
       0      to the start of the line
       ^      to the first non-blank of the line
       $      to the end of the line (with a count: count-1 lines
              further down)
       w      to the start of the next word; a word is a run of
              letters, digits and _, or a run of other non-blank
              characters
       b      back to the start of the word
       e      to the end of the word
       G      to line count (default: the last line)
       gg     to line count (default: the first line)
       H M L  to the top, middle or bottom line of the screen
       f c    forward to the count'th c on the line
       t c    forward to just before c
       F c    backward to c
       T c    backward to just after c
       ;      repeat the last f t F T
       ,      repeat it in the other direction
       %      to the bracket matching the first ( ) [ ] { } at or
              after the cursor on the line, across lines
       /re    search forward for the regular expression re,
              wrapping round past the end of the buffer; / alone
              repeats the last search
       ?re    search backward
       n      repeat the last search
       N      repeat it in the other direction
       ^F ^B  forward or back count screens
       ^D ^U  down or up half a screen

CHANGING
       i a    insert text before or after the cursor
       I A    insert text at the first non-blank or the end of the line
       o O    open a new line below or above and insert there
       R      replace: typed characters overwrite the line, and are
              added at its end
       x      delete count characters under and after the cursor
       X      delete count characters before the cursor
       r c    replace count characters with c
       ~      switch the case of count letters, moving right
       J      join count lines (default 2): leading blanks of the
              next line and trailing blanks of this one go, and one
              space comes between unless the next begins with )
       d m    delete from the cursor to the target of motion m (any
              of the moves above); dd deletes count lines
       c m    change: delete as d, then insert; cc changes count
              lines; cw is ce
       y m    yank (copy) into the buffer; yy yanks count lines
       D C    delete or change to the end of the line
       Y      yank count lines
       p P    put the yanked or deleted text after or before the
              cursor (lines: below or above the line), count times
       u      undo the last change; u again redoes it
       .      repeat the last change

       Motions j k G gg H M L and the doubled operators act on whole
       lines; the rest act on characters (e, f, t and % include the
       character they land on). A count may also come after the
       operator, as in d3w; the two counts multiply.

       In insert mode:

       ESC    leave insert mode
       return split the line
       ^H DEL erase the character before the cursor (not past the
              start of the line)
       ^W     erase the word before the cursor
       ^U     erase from the start of the line to the cursor
       ^V     take the next key literally

       Arrow keys are ignored while inserting.

OTHER KEYS
       ZZ     write the file if it was changed, and quit (as :x)
       ^L     redraw the screen
       ^G     show the file name, whether it is modified, the line
              number and the number of lines

EX COMMANDS
       A : command line is typed on the last row and ends with
       return. Backspace erases a character (and at the start of the
       line abandons it); ESC abandons it. A line address is a
       number, . (the cursor line) or $ (the last line), optionally
       followed by +n or -n. A range is one address, two separated by
       a comma, or % for the whole file. A range must lie within
       the buffer (else "Not that many lines in buffer") and be in
       order (else "Backwards range"); a command with a bad range
       does nothing. :0r reads at the top.

       :N     go to line N (any address alone)
       :w [file]
              write the buffer (or a range) to file, default the
              file being edited; it becomes the file's name if there
              was none. Only writing the whole buffer to the file
              being edited makes it unchanged for :q
       :wq [file]
              write, then quit
       :x     write if changed, then quit
       :q     quit; refused if the buffer was changed since the last
              write
       :q!    quit, discarding changes
       :e[!] [file]
              edit another file (default: the current one again);
              :e refuses if the buffer was changed, :e! discards the
              changes
       :r file
              read file in after the cursor line (or after the
              addressed line)
       :[range]d
              delete the lines (they can be put with p)
       :[range]s/re/text/[g]
              substitute text for the first match of re in each line
              (with g, every match). Any character may serve as the
              delimiter. In text & is the matched string and \c is a
              literal c. An empty re uses the last one. If no line
              matches nothing changes (and u still undoes the change
              before).
       :!cmd  run cmd with /bin/sh -c, then wait for a key; vi
              ignores interrupts only while cmd runs

REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
       c      an ordinary character matches itself
       \c     matches c literally
       .      any character
       [...]  any character in the set; a-z is a range, and a ] first
              in the set is literal
       [^...] any character not in the set
       *      zero or more of the preceding item, as many as possible
       ^      at the start: the beginning of the line
       $      at the end: the end of the line

       Searches and :s share the last regular expression.

EXAMPLES
       vi /etc/rc
       :%s/tty1/tty0/g        on every line, change tty1 to tty0
       :1,10d                 delete the first ten lines
       :w /usr/ken/rc.save    keep a copy
       ddp                    swap the line with the next one

ENVIRONMENT
       LINES     number of screen rows (more than 2; default 24)
       COLUMNS   number of screen columns (more than 10; default 80)
       HOME      where vi.recover is saved

       The terminal must understand the VT100 (ANSI) sequences for
       cursor addressing, clear screen and clear to end of line. vi
       puts it in raw mode, so ^C is an ordinary key.

       vi also reads keys from a file or pipe, so it can be driven by
       a script; the screen output is then of no use.

       On a hangup or terminate signal, or at the end of its input,
       vi saves a changed buffer in $HOME/vi.recover (or
       /tmp/vi.recover if HOME is not set), replacing any earlier
       one, says so on the standard error (vi: hangup: changes saved
       in /usr/ken/vi.recover) and exits with status 1. Get the text
       back with vi $HOME/vi.recover or :r.

FILES
       /bin/sh           runs :! commands
       $HOME/vi.recover  the buffer saved after a hangup

EXIT STATUS
       0 when vi quits. 1 if it runs out of memory, or on a hangup,
       a terminate signal or the end of its input with unwritten
       changes (they are saved, see above).

SEE ALSO
       ed(1), more(1), sh(1), stty(1)

BUGS
       Lines are limited to 4095 characters; a longer line is cut
       when the file is read, and typing stops at the limit. The :
       line is limited to 250 characters, patterns, replacements and
       file names to 255.

       There is one unnamed buffer: no named buffers ("a), marks (m,
       '), visual mode, :set, :map, :g, :n or :wq!, no W B E { } ( )
       motions, no s S >> <<, no ^E ^Y or z scrolling, no autoindent,
       and no search addresses (/re/) in ex ranges. H M L and ^D ^U
       ignore a count. The count before i a o and before . is
       ignored, and ~ is not repeated by . (a yank is not a change:
       . repeats the change before it). Undo is one level deep.

       An arrow
       key is recognised only when its three bytes arrive together,
       so a lone ESC followed quickly by [ may be taken for one.
       Backspace in insert mode does not join lines.
